llama.cpp
=========

Tracked as a git submodule at app/src/main/cpp/lib/llama.cpp. The pinned commit
IS the provenance -- `git submodule status` is authoritative, this file only
records what a bump involves.

Currently pinned to tag b10488.

Getting the source
------------------

    git clone --recurse-submodules <this repo>

or, in an existing clone:

    git submodule update --init

Add --shallow-submodules to the clone if you do not need llama.cpp's history.

Bumping the version
-------------------

    cd app/src/main/cpp/lib/llama.cpp
    git fetch --tags origin
    git checkout <new tag>
    cd -
    git add app/src/main/cpp/lib/llama.cpp

Then, before committing:

  1. Re-check llama_wrapper.cpp against include/llama.h. The C API changes
     between releases, and the compiler only catches some of it. The b10488
     bump needed three fixes:
       - llama_sampler_init_penalties() gained a leading n_vocab parameter
       - model_params.use_mmap / use_mlock became the load_mode enum
       - LLAMA_CURL was deprecated in favour of LLAMA_BUILD_APP / _UI / _MTMD
  2. Keep GGML_BACKEND_DL and GGML_CPU_ALL_VARIANTS OFF in ../CMakeLists.txt.
     Turning them on yields an APK with no compute backend at all: the backends
     become dlopen()ed modules searched for next to the executable, which on
     Android is /system/bin/app_process64 and never the APK.
  3. Delete llama.cpp/AGENTS.md and llama.cpp/CLAUDE.md. They are contributor
     instructions for llama.cpp itself and get picked up by tooling working in
     this repository. The submodule is configured with ignore = dirty so these
     deletions do not show up as changes.
  4. Run ./gradlew testDebugUnitTest connectedDebugAndroidTest. The instrumented
     suite is what proves the bump did not regress multi-turn behaviour; push a
     model to /data/local/tmp/test-model.gguf first.
